1339	  /**
1340	   * Returns the closest representable {@code long} to the absolute value of {@code x}.
1341	   *
1342	   * <p>This is the same thing as the true absolute value of {@code x} except in the case when
1343	   * {@code x} is {@link Long#MIN_VALUE}, in which case this returns {@link Long#MAX_VALUE}. (Note
1344	   * that {@code Long.MAX_VALUE} is mathematically equal to {@code -Long.MIN_VALUE - 1}.)
1345	   *
1346	   * <p>There are three common APIs for determining the absolute value of a long, all of which
1347	   * behave identically except when passed {@code Long.MIN_VALUE}. Those methods are:
1348	   *
1349	   * <ul>
1350	   *   <li>{@link Math#abs(long)}, which returns {@code Long.MIN_VALUE} when passed {@code
1351	   *       Long.MIN_VALUE}
1352	   *   <li>{@link Math#absExact(long)}, which throws {@link ArithmeticException} when passed {@code
1353	   *       Long.MIN_VALUE}
1354	   *   <li>this method, {@code LongMath.saturatedAbs(long)}, which returns {@code Long.MAX_VALUE}
1355	   *       when passed {@code Long.MIN_VALUE}
1356	   * </ul>
1357	   *
1358	   * <p>Note that if your only goal is to turn a well-distributed {@code long} (such as a random
1359	   * number) into a well-distributed nonnegative number, the most even distribution is achieved not
1360	   * by this method or other absolute value methods, but by {@code x & Long.MAX_VALUE}.
1361	   *
1362	   * @since 33.5.0
1363	   */
1364	  public static long saturatedAbs(long x) {
1365	    return (x == Long.MIN_VALUE) ? Long.MAX_VALUE : Math.abs(x);
1366	  }
