253	   * Returns a splitter that divides strings into pieces of the given length. For example, {@code
254	   * Splitter.fixedLength(2).split("abcde")} returns an iterable containing {@code ["ab", "cd",
255	   * "e"]}. The last piece can be smaller than {@code length} but will never be empty.
256	   *
257	   * <p><b>Note:</b> if {@link #fixedLength} is used in conjunction with {@link #limit}, the final
258	   * split piece <i>may be longer than the specified fixed length</i>. This is because the splitter
259	   * will <i>stop splitting when the limit is reached</i>, and just return the final piece as-is.
260	   *
261	   * <p><b>Exception:</b> for consistency with separator-based splitters, {@code split("")} does not
262	   * yield an empty iterable, but an iterable containing {@code ""}. This is the only case in which
263	   * {@code Iterables.size(split(input))} does not equal {@code IntMath.divide(input.length(),
264	   * length, CEILING)}. To avoid this behavior, use {@code omitEmptyStrings}.
265	   *
266	   * @param length the desired length of pieces after splitting, a positive integer
267	   * @return a splitter, with default settings, that can split into fixed sized pieces
268	   * @throws IllegalArgumentException if {@code length} is zero or negative
